          "sourceDoi": "10.1257/aer.20180338",
          "targetDoi": "10.1007/s00199-006-0124-4",
          "snippet": "Alternatively, there are other possible explanations for declining research productivity. Incumbent firms may be shifting to \"defensive\" R&amp;D to protect their market position, and this could cause research productivity to decline; Dinopoulos and Syropoulos (2007) provide a model along these lines. Or perhaps declines in basic research spending (potentially related to the U.S. decline in publicly-funded research as a share of GDP) have negatively impacted overall research productivity.",
          "section": "results",
          "type": "mentioning"
        },
        {
          "sourceDoi": "10.1257/aer.20180338",
          "targetDoi": "10.1016/b978-0-7506-7009-8.50019-7",
          "snippet": "Others have also provided evidence suggesting that ideas may be getting harder to find over time. Griliches (1994) provides a summary of the earlier literature exploring the decline in patents per dollar of research spending. Gordon (2016) reports extensive new historical evidence from throughout the 19th and 20th centuries.",
          "section": "introduction",
          "type": "mentioning"
        },
        {
          "sourceDoi": "10.1257/aer.20180338",
          "targetDoi": "10.1016/j.red.2009.12.002",
          "snippet": "Finally, this analysis can be applied across different firms, goods, or industries, following the insights of Ngai and Samaniego (2011), who develop a semi-endogenous growth model with heterogeneity in the dynamic spillover parameters of the idea production functions. Some goods, like semiconductors, can have rapid productivity growth because their β is small, while other goods like the speed of airplanes or perhaps the education industry itself could have slow rates of innovation because their β is large.",
          "section": "discussion",
          "type": "mentioning"
        }
      ],
